**Sec. 71101.** The Secretary of Health and Human Services is prohibited from implementing, administering, or enforcing specific amendments to Medicare Savings Program eligibility and enrollment rules (88 Fed. Reg. 65230) from the date of enactment through September 30, 2034. This moratorium applies to five specific sections of title 42 of the Code of Federal Regulations. The section appropriates $1,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 to carry out this section and Section 71102.

**Sec. 71102.** The Secretary of Health and Human Services is barred from implementing, administering, or enforcing amendments to Medicaid, CHIP, and Basic Health Program application and enrollment rules (89 Fed. Reg. 22780) from the date of enactment through September 30, 2034. The moratorium covers numerous specific sections within Parts 431, 435, 447, and 457 of title 42 of the Code of Federal Regulations.

**Sec. 71103.** States must establish processes to regularly obtain address information for Medicaid enrollees beginning January 1, 2027, using reliable sources such as the USPS National Change of Address Database. By October 1, 2029, the Secretary must establish a federal system to prevent individuals from being simultaneously enrolled in Medicaid plans in multiple states. States must submit Social Security numbers and other data to this system monthly to identify and disenroll individuals who do not reside in the state. The section appropriates $10,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and $20,000,000 for fiscal year 2029 for system establishment and maintenance.

**Sec. 71104.** Beginning January 1, 2027, states must quarterly screen the Death Master File to identify deceased individuals enrolled in Medicaid. If an individual is identified as deceased, the state must treat the information as factual, disenroll the individual, and discontinue payments for services furnished after death. States must immediately reinstate coverage retroactively if an individual was erroneously disenrolled due to a misidentification in the Death Master File.

**Sec. 71105.** Beginning January 1, 2028, states must check the Death Master File during the enrollment, reenrollment, or revalidation of any Medicaid provider or supplier. This check must also be conducted not less frequently than quarterly while the provider or supplier remains enrolled. The purpose is to ensure that deceased providers do not remain enrolled in the Medicaid program.

**Sec. 71106.** Effective for fiscal year 2030, the section amends the rules for waiving erroneous excess payments under Medicaid. The Secretary may waive amounts only for audits conducted by the Secretary or, at the Secretary's option, by the State. The amount waived in any fiscal year cannot exceed the erroneous excess payments that exceed the allowable error rate of 0.03. The definition of erroneous excess payments is expanded to include payments where insufficient information is available to confirm eligibility.

**Sec. 71107.** For redeterminations scheduled on or after the first day of the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026, states must conduct eligibility redeterminations every six months for individuals enrolled under the Medicaid expansion. This requirement does not apply to individuals who are exempt under specific subsections regarding certain categories of enrollees. The Secretary must issue implementation guidance within 180 days of enactment. The section appropriates $75,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71108.** Effective January 1, 2028, states may elect to apply a higher home equity limit for Medicaid long-term care eligibility, up to a maximum of $1,000,000, for homes not zoned for agricultural use. For homes on lots zoned for agricultural use, the existing limit applies. The section clarifies that states cannot determine eligibility for nursing facility or long-term care services without applying the home equity interest limit.

**Sec. 71109.** Beginning October 1, 2026, federal Medicaid payments will not be made for medical assistance furnished to individuals who are not U.S. citizens, nationals, lawfully admitted permanent residents, Cuban/Haitian entrants, or individuals residing under a Compact of Free Association. This restriction applies to the 50 states and the District of Columbia. The section appropriates $15,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71110.** Beginning October 1, 2026, the Federal Medical Assistance Percentage (FMAP) for emergency Medicaid services furnished to certain aliens will not exceed the standard FMAP determined for the state. This provision overrides previous higher FMAP rates for emergency care for these individuals. The section appropriates $1,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71111.** The Secretary of Health and Human Services is prohibited from implementing, administering, or enforcing minimum staffing standards for long-term care facilities under Medicare and Medicaid (89 Fed. Reg. 40876) from the date of enactment through September 30, 2034. The moratorium specifically applies to Sections 483.5 and 483.35 of Part 483 of title 42 of the Code of Federal Regulations.

**Sec. 71112.** For applications made on or after the first day of the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026, Medicaid coverage for expansion individuals will be retroactive only to the month before the application month. For all other individuals, coverage will be retroactive only to the second month before the application month. This change reduces the previous three-month retroactive coverage period. The section appropriates $10,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71113.** No federal Medicaid funds may be used to make payments to "prohibited entities" for items and services furnished during the one-year period beginning on the date of enactment. A prohibited entity is defined as a 501(c)(3) organization or essential community provider that provides abortions (with limited exceptions for rape, incest, or life-endangering conditions) and received more than $800,000 in federal and state Medicaid expenditures in fiscal year 2023. The section appropriates $1,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71114.** The increased Federal Medical Assistance Percentage (FMAP) incentive for states that provide Medicaid coverage to all expansion individuals is sunset if the state begins expending amounts for all such individuals prior to January 1, 2026. This amendment modifies the conditions under which the enhanced FMAP rate applies.

**Sec. 71115.** For fiscal years beginning on or after October 1, 2026, the hold harmless threshold for broad-based health care related taxes is reduced from 6 percent to a lower "applicable percent." For expansion states, this applicable percent decreases annually from 5.5 percent in fiscal year 2028 to 3.5 percent in fiscal year 2032 and subsequent years. Non-expansion states are subject to the threshold determined as of the date of enactment. The section appropriates $20,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71116.** The total payment rate for state directed payments to managed care entities is limited to 100 percent of the specified total published Medicare payment rate for expansion states, and 110 percent for non-expansion states. Grandfathered payments approved before May 1, 2025, or for rural hospitals, will be reduced by 10 percentage points each year starting January 1, 2028, until they reach the new limits. The section appropriates $7,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2026 through 2033 for implementation.

**Sec. 71117.** The section tightens the definition of "generally redistributive" for Medicaid provider taxes by specifying conditions under which a tax is not considered redistributive. A tax is not redistributive if it imposes lower rates on taxpayers with lower Medicaid taxable units, higher rates on Medicaid units than non-Medicaid units, or excludes groups based on characteristics that approximate Medicaid usage. The amendments take effect upon enactment, subject to a transition period not exceeding three fiscal years.

**Sec. 71118.** Beginning January 1, 2027, the Secretary may not approve a Medicaid demonstration project unless the Chief Actuary certifies that the project will not result in an increase in federal expenditures compared to the absence of the project. If expenditures are lower than expected, the Secretary must specify a methodology to account for the difference in subsequent approval periods. The section appropriates $5,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2026 and 2027 for implementation.

**Sec. 71119.** Beginning not later than the first day of the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026, states must require "applicable individuals" (generally expansion enrollees aged 19-64) to demonstrate community engagement, such as working 80 hours, performing 80 hours of community service, or being enrolled in education, as a condition of eligibility. Exceptions exist for specific excluded individuals, including those under 19, pregnant, disabled, or veterans with total disability. States must provide notice and a 30-day cure period for noncompliance before denying or disenrolling individuals. The section appropriates $200,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services and $200,000,000 in grants to states for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 71120.** Beginning October 1, 2028, states must impose cost sharing (deductions or similar charges) on "specified individuals" (expansion enrollees with income above the poverty line) for certain care, items, or services. No enrollment fees or premiums may be imposed on these individuals. Cost sharing for any single item or service cannot exceed $35, and total family cost sharing cannot exceed 5 percent of family income on a monthly or quarterly basis. The section appropriates $15,000,000 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71121.** Beginning July 1, 2028, the Secretary may approve standalone waivers for home and community-based services (HCBS) for individuals who do not meet the standard institutional level of care determination. States must establish needs-based criteria for eligibility and ensure that per capita expenditures for these individuals do not exceed those for individuals receiving institutional care. The section appropriates $50,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and $100,000,000 for fiscal year 2027 for implementation and state system support.

**Sec. 71201.** An individual may be entitled to or enrolled for Medicare benefits only if they are a U.S. citizen, national, lawfully admitted permanent resident, Cuban/Haitian entrant, or individual residing under a Compact of Free Association. For individuals currently enrolled, this restriction applies 18 months after the date of enactment. The Commissioner of Social Security must review current enrollees within one year of enactment to identify those who do not meet these criteria and notify them of termination.

**Sec. 71202.** The Medicare Physician Fee Schedule payment rate is increased by 2.5 percent for services furnished on or after January 1, 2026, and before January 1, 2027. This temporary increase accounts for exceptional circumstances and extends the previous temporary payment increases that applied through 2024.

**Sec. 71203.** The exclusion for orphan drugs under the Drug Price Negotiation Program is expanded to include drugs approved for one or more rare diseases, rather than only one. The section also clarifies the treatment of former orphan drugs, extending the exclusion period until the first day after the drug is no longer considered an orphan drug. These amendments apply to initial price applicability years beginning on or after January 1, 2028.

**Sec. 71301.** The premium tax credit is restricted to individuals who are not lawfully present or, if lawfully present, are "eligible aliens." Eligible aliens are defined as lawfully admitted permanent residents, Cuban/Haitian entrants, or individuals residing under a Compact of Free Association. These amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026, and plan years beginning on or after January 1, 2027.

**Sec. 71302.** The premium tax credit is disallowed for any month in which an individual is ineligible for Medicaid solely due to their alien status. This amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71303.** A "coverage month" for premium tax credit purposes does not include any month before the Exchange verifies the individual's eligibility to enroll and for advance payments. Exchanges must provide a pre-enrollment verification process by August 1 for the subsequent plan year. These amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2027.

**Sec. 71304.** The premium tax credit is disallowed for plans enrolled in during a special enrollment period based on household income relative to the poverty line, unless the enrollment is connected to a qualifying life event or change in circumstances specified by the Secretary of Health and Human Services. This amendment applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71305.** The limitation on the recapture of advance payments of the premium tax credit is eliminated. This means that if an individual receives more in advance payments than their actual credit, the full excess amount is subject to recapture. This amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71306.** The safe harbor allowing high deductible health plans (HDHPs) to have no deductible for telehealth and other remote care services is made permanent. This amendment applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 71307.** Bronze and catastrophic plans available as individual coverage through an Exchange are treated as high deductible health plans (HDHPs) for purposes of Health Savings Account (HSA) eligibility. This amendment applies to months beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71308.** Direct primary care service arrangements, where an individual pays a fixed periodic fee for primary care services, are not treated as health plans for HDHP purposes. Fees for such arrangements are treated as medical expenses for HSA contribution purposes, provided the aggregate monthly fees do not exceed $150 ($300 for family coverage). This amendment applies to months beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71401.** The section establishes the Rural Health Transformation Program, appropriating $10,000,000,000 per year for fiscal years 2026 through 2030 to states. States must submit a detailed rural health transformation plan to be eligible for allotments, which are distributed 50 percent equally and 50 percent based on rural population and facility metrics. Funds must be used for at least three specified activities, such as improving access, promoting technology, or recruiting workforce, and no state matching funds are required.

**Sec. 72001.** The limitation on the public debt under section 3101(b) of title 31, United States Code, is increased by $5,000,000,000,000.

**Sec. 73001.** No federal funds may be used to pay unemployment compensation benefits to an individual whose wages during the base period equal or exceed $1,000,000. Applicants must self-certify that their wages do not exceed this threshold, and state agencies must verify wage eligibility to the degree possible. Individuals who receive benefits to which they were not entitled must repay the amounts. This prohibition applies to weeks of unemployment beginning on or after the date of enactment. [end of text]


